WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:03.080
This environment was Ready.

2
00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:04.600
It was Deployable.

3
00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.440
And it held no reference data at all.

4
00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.400
Six minutes and one command later:

5
00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:17.470
80,103 records. Ready is

6
00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:18.360
not loaded.

7
00:00:25.332 --> 00:00:29.452
Fieldnote ten. Ready is not loaded.

8
00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:33.670
When is a new environment
ready, open for deploys,

9
00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:34.880
and holding data?

10
00:00:36.352 --> 00:00:38.122
Ready is Flux's verdict.

11
00:00:38.352 --> 00:00:41.082
Deployable adds the
environment's own gates.

12
00:00:41.472 --> 00:00:45.232
And loading data is a separate
step you run with spi load,

13
00:00:45.232 --> 00:00:46.682
which changes neither.

14
00:00:48.566 --> 00:00:51.376
spi status answers two questions.

15
00:00:51.606 --> 00:00:55.376
Ready means every gating
Flux layer has converged.

16
00:00:55.606 --> 00:00:59.486
Layers marked non-gating, like
legal seeding and DAG registration,

17
00:00:59.486 --> 00:01:01.216
are shown but never count.

18
00:01:01.366 --> 00:01:03.456
Deployable adds three more:

19
00:01:03.606 --> 00:01:07.006
maintenance is off, the
deploy record exists,

20
00:01:07.006 --> 00:01:10.416
and the members Job has
finished in every partition.

21
00:01:11.126 --> 00:01:13.206
This environment came up at a release tag,

22
00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:15.136
so it started in maintenance.

23
00:01:15.366 --> 00:01:18.976
At 30 of 30 it was Ready,
and not deployable.

24
00:01:19.126 --> 00:01:22.096
After a lookup answered,
maintenance was cleared,

25
00:01:22.166 --> 00:01:24.016
and it read Deployable.

26
00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:29.540
Bring-up registers the schemas,
and writes no records.

27
00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:33.140
That load is Flux's Job,
behind a gating layer.

28
00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:36.650
Reference data, from the
community's data definitions,

29
00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:40.170
is loaded by nothing
until you run spi load.

30
00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:43.540
The stack version ships a
registry of named loads,

31
00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:46.010
pins this one to a release and its commit,

32
00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:48.100
and makes it wait for the schemas.

33
00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:52.820
And spi load refuses to start until
the environment is Deployable.

34
00:01:55.118 --> 00:01:58.328
spi load created one
Job for the partition.

35
00:01:58.638 --> 00:02:02.328
It checked that all 584
kinds were registered,

36
00:02:02.398 --> 00:02:06.248
then wrote in batches of
500, skipping duplicates.

37
00:02:06.638 --> 00:02:11.768
80,103 records, none failed,
in about six minutes.

38
00:02:11.998 --> 00:02:14.248
Complete means Storage accepted them.

39
00:02:14.478 --> 00:02:20.248
The indexer may still be working, and a
load never changes Ready or Deployable.

40
00:02:22.746 --> 00:02:24.596
Follow one environment from Ready.

41
00:02:25.066 --> 00:02:27.706
Status says Ready, not deployable:

42
00:02:27.706 --> 00:02:32.116
maintenance. You clear it,
and the deploy record says so.

43
00:02:32.666 --> 00:02:35.786
spi load checks Deployable
and the schemas,

44
00:02:35.786 --> 00:02:37.636
and creates the load Job.

45
00:02:38.026 --> 00:02:42.916
The Job fetches the data definitions at
a pinned commit and checks every kind.

46
00:02:43.226 --> 00:02:47.316
Storage accepts 80,103 records.

47
00:02:47.986 --> 00:02:51.316
And spi load --status reads complete.

48
00:02:53.926 --> 00:02:55.086
An easy mistake:

49
00:02:55.086 --> 00:02:58.136
it's Deployable, so the data is there.

50
00:02:58.366 --> 00:03:01.336
Deployable says the
environment can take a deploy.

51
00:03:01.646 --> 00:03:06.536
Reference data is a separate load, and
a load never changes either verdict.

52
00:03:06.766 --> 00:03:10.216
So a search suite on a fresh
environment can find nothing,

53
00:03:10.286 --> 00:03:13.336
and nothing says why,
unless you read the loads.

54
00:03:13.566 --> 00:03:17.336
Check with spi load --status.

55
00:03:19.682 --> 00:03:21.092
So now you can say:

56
00:03:21.242 --> 00:03:25.492
Deployable is Ready, with maintenance
off and bootstrap finished.

57
00:03:25.882 --> 00:03:29.492
A Deployable environment
holds schemas and no records.

58
00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:32.602
And loaded means Storage
accepted the records,

59
00:03:32.602 --> 00:03:34.452
not that search finds them.

60
00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:41.330
Next: how does a DAG reach the
stack, who does each task act as,

61
00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.820
and how do you know it stored anything?

